      "claim": "The U.S. Constitution vests the executive power of the United States in the President, and only the President may originate executive orders.",
      "evidence": "Article II, §1 of the U.S. Constitution; longstanding constitutional interpretation that executive orders are a presidential prerogative.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Federal law defines an executive order as a directive issued by the President or by persons acting under authority expressly delegated by the President.",
      "evidence": "5 U.S.C. § 301 and related statutes; case law (e.g., *Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer*) interpreting presidential directives.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Delegated authority to issue executive orders can be granted only to officers of the United States, and officers must be U.S. citizens.",
      "evidence": "5 U.S.C. § 3326 requires U.S. citizenship for federal officers; agency regulations confirm citizenship as a prerequisite for appointment to positions that can receive presidential delegation.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "John Key holds no U.S. federal office and is not a U.S. citizen.",
      "evidence": "Public records show no appointment of John Key to any U.S. executive‑branch position; citizenship records indicate he remains solely a New Zealand citizen.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Because John Key lacks both the constitutional authority of the President and any delegated statutory authority as a U.S. officer, he cannot issue an executive order in the United States.",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 1‑5: executive orders are limited to the President or delegated U.S. officers, a status John Key does not possess.",
